## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to unpack bit-packed fields without branches—a common low-level task in networking, codecs, and embedded systems.

## Key Concepts

- **Bitwise masks and shifts** extract subfields from packed integers efficiently
- **No branches** ensures constant-time execution, critical for performance-sensitive code
- **Endianness** is irrelevant when masking at exact bit positions

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
    uint8_t  version;
    uint8_t  type;
    uint16_t length;
    uint8_t  flags;
} Header;

Header extractHeaderFields(uint32_t w) {
    Header h;
    h.version = (w >> 28) & 0xF;  // Bits 31-28
    h.type    = (w >> 24) & 0xF;  // Bits 27-24
    h.length  = (w >> 8)  & 0xFFFF; // Bits 23-8
    h.flags   = w & 0xFF;          // Bits 7-0
    return h;
}
```

Each shift moves the target field to the least significant position, then the mask isolates the relevant bits. This approach is branchless, deterministic, and executes in constant time.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests precise bit permutation across 128-bit boundary without loops. Requires careful cross-half carry handling when shifting hi/lo.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value as {hi, lo}
- Masks for even/odd bit positions across both halves
- Cross-half carry during shifts between lo and hi

## Code Example

```javascript
// Outline (not full implementation)
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 evenMask = {0x5555555555555555ULL, 0x5555555555555555ULL};
  uint128 oddMask  = {0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL, 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL};
  uint64_t lo_e = x.lo & evenMask.lo; uint64_t hi_e = x.hi & evenMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_o = x.lo & oddMask.lo;  uint64_t hi_o = x.hi & oddMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_left  = (lo_e << 1);
  uint64_t hi_left  = (hi_e << 1) | (lo_e >> 63);
  uint64_t lo_right = (lo_o >> 1);
  uint64_t hi_right = (hi_o >> 1);
  uint128 res;
  res.lo = lo_left | lo_right;
  res.hi = hi_left | hi_right;
  // handle cross-half carry from lo_o/hi_o if needed
  return res;
}
```

