## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of fixed-depth carry logic and 128-bit arithmetic without loops—critical in high-throughput code paths.

## Key Concepts

- **Carry-lookahead across 4×32-bit lanes**: Break 128 bits into four 32-bit chunks for parallel carry computation.
- **Propagate (P) and Generate (G) signals**: P = a ^ b (carry passes through), G = a & b (carry generated).
- **Fixed-depth carry computation**: Uses only OR/AND operations, achieving O(1) depth.
- **Correct 128-bit carry propagation**: Ensure carries propagate correctly across all four lanes.

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 add128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
    uint64_t a0 = (uint32_t)a.lo;
    uint64_t a1 = (uint32_t)(a.lo >> 32);
    uint64_t a2 = (uint32_t)a.hi;
    uint64_t a3 = (uint32_t)(a.hi >> 32);

    uint64_t b0 = (uint32_t)b.lo;
    uint64_t b1 = (uint32_t)(b.lo >> 32);
    uint64_t b2 = (uint32_t)b.hi;
    uint64_t b3 = (uint32_t)(b.hi >> 32);

    uint64_t p0 = a0 ^ b0, g0 = a0 & b0;
    uint64_t p1 = a1 ^ b1, g1 = a1 & b1;
    uint64_t p2 = a2 ^ b2, g2 = a2 & b2;
    uint64_t p3 = a3 ^ b3, g3 = a3 & b3;

    uint64_t c1 = g0;
    uint64_t c2 = g0 | (p0 & g1);
    uint64_t c3 = g0 | (p0 & g1) | (p0 & p1 & g2);
    uint64_t c4 = g0 | (p0 & g1) | (p0 & p1 & g2) | (p0 & p1 & p2 & g3);

    uint128 r;
    r.lo = (p3 ^ c4) | ((p2 ^ c3) << 32) | ((p1 ^ c2) << 64) | ((p0 ^ c1) << 96);
    r.hi = (p3 ^ c4) >> 32;
    return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless thinking and lane-wise masking, fundamental techniques in low-level libraries and SIMD operations.

## Key Concepts
- Lane-wise masking without conditionals using two's complement arithmetic
- 128-bit composition from 32-bit chunks
- Bitwise extraction and packing operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode C-like implementation demonstrating the core idea
uint128 blendMask128(uint128 x){
  uint32_t a3 = (x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a2 = x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a1 = (x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a0 = x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint64_t m3 = -(uint64_t)(a3 & 1);
  uint64_t m2 = -(uint64_t)(a2 & 1);
  uint64_t m1 = -(uint64_t)(a1 & 1);
  uint64_t m0 = -(uint64_t)(a0 & 1);
  uint128 result;
  result.hi = (m3 << 32) | m2;
  result.lo = (m1 << 32) | m0;
  return result;
}
```

## Quick Tests
- Input: 0x123456789ABCDEF0 → Output: 0x0000000000000000 (all lanes even)
- Input: 0x12345679_9ABCDEF1 → Output: 0xFFFFFFFF_00000000 (only A0 odd)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit arithmetic using hi/lo, avoiding loops, and applying a scalable, branchless popcount.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo
- SWAR/parallel bit counting
- Endianness and safe casting between types

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x -= (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x){
  return (uint8_t)(popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo));
}
``` 

