## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reduce a 128-bit input to a single bit using only shifts and XORs, a common low-level optimization pattern. It emphasizes branchless, predictable timing.

## Key Concepts
- XOR folding to accumulate parity across halves
- Branchless, constant-time bitwise manipulation
- Handling 128-bit input via hi/lo halves without loops

## Code Example
```javascript
function parity128(hi, lo) {
  let p = hi ^ lo;
  p = p ^ (p >>> 32);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 16);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 8);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 4);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 2);
  p = p ^ (p >>> 1);
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 256-bit values?
- What's the performance impact of using XOR vs. addition for parity?
- Can you implement this using only addition and subtraction?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked

- Tests ability to implement a canonical bit-counting trick without loops, emphasizing constant-time operations. 
- Demonstrates careful use of masks and shifts to aggregate counts across bit groups.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (parallel bit counting)
- Bit masks 0x55555555, 0x33333333, 0x0F0F0F0F
- Constant-time arithmetic with shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
function popcount32(x) {
  x = x - ((x >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >>> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  return (x * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
}
```

