## Why This Is Asked
Tests crossing-boundary bit extraction on a 128-bit value with no branching, mirroring real-world crypto/low-level data parsing tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-boundary bit manipulation.
- Mask generation for widths up to 64 without 64-bit overflow.
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves (no loops).

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractBits128(hi, lo, pos, width) {
  const V = (BigInt(hi) << 64n) | BigInt(lo);
  const mask = width === 64 ? (1n << 64n) - 1n : (1n << BigInt(width)) - 1n;
  return (V >> BigInt(pos)) & mask;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to count 1-runs in a 64-bit word using bit-twiddling with no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Start mask n & ~(n << 1) identifies 1-run starts
- Parallel popcount technique to count bits
- Edge cases: all zeros, runs spanning MSB

## Code Example
```cpp
uint64_t countOneRuns64(uint64_t n){
  uint64_t starts = n & ~(n << 1);
  uint64_t x = starts;
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit operands?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based scan in terms of latency and pipeline efficiency.

## Why This Is Asked

