## Why This Is Asked
Advanced bit manipulation questions assess knowledge of branchless arithmetic and de Bruijn indexing techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Bit manipulation: isolating the least significant bit using two's complement arithmetic
- De Bruijn sequence mapping for constant-time position lookup
- Branchless, constant-time operations
- Proper edge-case handling for n == 0

## Code Example
```c
int ctz64(uint64_t n) {
  // Implementation uses de Bruijn indexing
  uint64_t t = n & -n;
  // table[index] yields trailing-zero count
  return table[(t * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a01ULL) >> 58];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform cross-128-bit bit counting without loops, using portable bit-twiddling. Demonstrates knowledge of XOR for difference, and multi-word popcount without hardware intrinsics.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based difference
- SWAR-style popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining counts for 128-bit data

## Code Example

```c
unsigned distance128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  uint64_t x = a.hi ^ b.hi;
  uint64_t y = a.lo ^ b.lo;
  // SWAR popcount on x
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  // SWAR popcount on y
  y = y - ((y >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  y = (y & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((y >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  y = (y + (y >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = y + (y >> 8);
  y = y + (y >> 16);
  y = y + (y >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0xFF) + (unsigned)(y & 0xFF);
}
```

